sn12.18:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Staying near Sāvatthī …
sn12.18:1.2 | S: Then the wanderer Timbaruka went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Timbarukkha the wanderer went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
sn12.18:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, Timbarukkha the wanderer said to the Blessed One,
sn12.18:2.1 | S: “Well, worthy Gotama, are pleasure and pain made by oneself?” | T: “Now, then, Master Gotama, are pleasure & pain self-made?”
sn12.18:2.2 | S: “Not so, Timbaruka,” said the Buddha. | T: “Don’t say that, Timbarukkha,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.18:2.3 | S: “Then are pleasure and pain made by another?” | T: “Then are pleasure & pain other-made?”
sn12.18:2.4 | S: “Not so, Timbaruka,” said the Buddha. | T: “Don’t say that, Timbarukkha,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.18:2.5 | S: “Well, are pleasure and pain made by both oneself and another?” | T: “Then are pleasure & pain self-made & other-made?”
sn12.18:2.6 | S: “Not so, Timbaruka,” said the Buddha. | T: “Don’t say that, Timbarukkha,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.18:2.7 | S: “Then do pleasure and pain arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another?” | T: “Then are pleasure & pain, without self-making or other-making, spontaneously arisen?”
sn12.18:2.8 | S: “Not so, Timbaruka,” said the Buddha. | T: “Don’t say that, Timbarukkha,” the Blessed One said.
sn12.18:2.9 | S: “Well, is there no such thing as pleasure and pain?” | T: “Then is there no pleasure & pain?”
sn12.18:2.10 | S: “It’s not that there’s no such thing as pleasure and pain. | T: “It’s not the case that there is no pleasure & pain, Timbarukkha.
sn12.18:2.11 | S: Pleasure and pain are real.” | T: There is pleasure & pain.”
