sn12.18:3.10 | S: Sir, explain pleasure and pain to me! | T: Then tell me about pleasure & pain, Master Gotama.
sn12.18:3.11 | S: Teach me about pleasure and pain!” | T: Teach me about pleasure & pain.”
sn12.18:4.1 | S: “Suppose that the feeling and the one who feels it are one and the same. Then for one who has existed since the beginning, pleasure and pain is made by oneself. I don’t say this. | T: “Timbarukkha, I don’t say that—with the feeling being the same as the one who feels, existing from the beginning—pleasure & pain are self-made.
sn12.18:4.2 | S: Suppose that the feeling is one thing and the one who feels it is another. Then for one stricken by feeling, pleasure and pain is made by another. I don’t say this. | T: And I don’t say that—with feeling being one thing and the one who feels another, existing as the one struck by the feeling—pleasure & pain are other-made.
sn12.18:4.3 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way: | T: Avoiding these two extremes, the Tathāgata teaches the Dhamma via the middle:
sn12.18:4.4 | S: ‘Ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: From ignorance as a requisite condition come fabrications.
sn12.18:4.5 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: From fabrications as a requisite condition comes consciousness. From consciousness as a requisite condition comes name-&-form. From name-&-form as a requisite condition come the six sense media. From the six sense media as a requisite condition comes contact. From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling. From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving. From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance. From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming. From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth. From birth as a requisite condition, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.18:4.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.18:4.7 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: “Now from the remainderless fading & cessation of that very ignorance comes the cessation of fabrications.
sn12.18:4.8 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: From the cessation of fabrications comes the cessation of consciousness. From the cessation of consciousness comes the cessation of name-&-form. From the cessation of name-&-form comes the cessation of the six sense media. From the cessation of the six sense media comes the cessation of contact. From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of feeling. From the cessation of feeling comes the cessation of craving. From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance. From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.18:4.9 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.’” | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.”
sn12.18:5.1 | S: When he said this, the wanderer Timbaruka said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, Timbarukkha the wanderer said to the Blessed One:
sn12.18:5.2 | S: “Excellent, worthy Gotama! Excellent! … | T: “Magnificent, Master Gotama! Magnificent! Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has Master Gotama—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear.
sn12.18:5.3 | S: I go for refuge to the worthy Gotama, to the teaching, and to the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: I go to Master Gotama for refuge, to the Dhamma, & to the Saṅgha of monks.
sn12.18:5.4 | S: From this day forth, may the worthy Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: May Master Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge from this day forward, for life.”
sn12.18:5.5 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.20
sn12.20:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Staying near Sāvatthī …
