## sn12.38
sn12.38:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Staying near Sāvatthī …
sn12.38:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, what you intend or plan, and what you have underlying tendencies for become a support for the continuation of consciousness. | T: (the Blessed One said,) “Monks, what one intends, what one arranges, and what one obsesses about: This is a support for the stationing of consciousness.
sn12.38:1.3 | S: When this support exists, consciousness becomes established. | T: There being a support, there is a landing of consciousness.
sn12.38:1.4 | S: When consciousness is established and grows, there is regeneration into a new state of existence in the future. | T: When that consciousness lands and grows, there is the production of renewed becoming in the future.
sn12.38:1.5 | S: When there is regeneration into a new state of existence in the future, future rebirth, old age, and death come to be, as do sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: When there is the production of renewed becoming in the future, there is future birth, aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair.
sn12.38:1.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of suffering & stress.
sn12.38:2.1 | S: If you don’t intend or plan, but still have underlying tendencies, this becomes a support for the continuation of consciousness. | T: “If one doesn’t intend and doesn’t arrange, but one still obsesses (about something), this is a support for the stationing of consciousness.
sn12.38:2.2 | S: When this support exists, consciousness becomes established. | T: There being a support, there is a landing of consciousness.
sn12.38:2.3 | S: When consciousness is established and grows, there is regeneration into a new state of existence in the future. | T: When that consciousness lands and grows, there is the production of renewed becoming in the future.
sn12.38:2.4 | S: When there is regeneration into a new state of existence in the future, future rebirth, old age, and death come to be, as do sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: When there is the production of renewed becoming in the future, there is future birth, aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair.
sn12.38:2.5 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such (too) is the origination of this entire mass of suffering & stress.
sn12.38:3.1 | S: If you don’t intend or plan or have underlying tendencies, this doesn’t become a support for the continuation of consciousness. | T: “But when one doesn’t intend, arrange, or obsess (about anything), there is no support for the stationing of consciousness.
sn12.38:3.2 | S: With no support, consciousness is not established. | T: There being no support, there is no landing of consciousness.
sn12.38:3.3 | S: When consciousness is not established and doesn’t grow, there’s no regeneration into a new state of existence in the future. | T: When that consciousness doesn’t land & grow, there is no production of renewed becoming in the future.
sn12.38:3.4 | S: When there is no regeneration into a new state of existence in the future, future rebirth, old age, and death cease, as do sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: When there is no production of renewed becoming in the future, there is no future birth, aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, or despair.
sn12.38:3.5 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.” | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of suffering & stress.”
sn12.38:3.6 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.44
sn12.44:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn12.44:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you the origin and disappearance of the world. | T: There the Blessed One addressed the monks: “I will teach you the origination of the world & the ending of the world.
sn12.44:1.3 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
sn12.44:1.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to the Blessed One.
sn12.44:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said:
sn12.44:2.1 | S: “And what, mendicants, is the origin of the world? | T: “And what is the origination of the world?
sn12.44:2.2 | S: Eye consciousness arises dependent on the eye and sights. The meeting of the three is contact. | T: Dependent on the eye & forms there arises eye-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.
sn12.44:2.3 | S: Contact is a requirement for feeling. | T: From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling.
sn12.44:2.4 | S: Feeling is a requirement for craving. | T: From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving.
sn12.44:2.5 | S: Craving is a requirement for grasping. | T: From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance.
sn12.44:2.6 | S: Grasping is a requirement for continued existence. | T: From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming.
sn12.44:2.7 | S: Continued existence is a requirement for rebirth. | T: From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth.
sn12.44:2.8 | S: Rebirth is a requirement for old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress to come to be. | T: From birth as a requisite condition, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.44:2.9 | S: This is the origin of the world. | T: This is the origination of the world.
sn12.44:3.1 | S: Ear consciousness arises dependent on the ear and sounds. … | T: “Dependent on the ear & sounds there arises ear-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.…
sn12.44:3.2 | S: Nose consciousness arises dependent on the nose and smells. … | T: Dependent on the nose & aromas there arises nose-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.…
sn12.44:3.3 | S: Tongue consciousness arises dependent on the tongue and tastes. … | T: Dependent on the tongue & flavors there arises tongue-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.…
sn12.44:3.4 | S: Body consciousness arises dependent on the body and touches. … | T: Dependent on the body & tactile sensations there arises body-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.…
sn12.44:3.5 | S: Mind consciousness arises dependent on the mind and ideas. The meeting of the three is contact. | T: Dependent on the intellect & mental qualities there arises intellect-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.
sn12.44:3.6 | S: Contact is a requirement for feeling. … | T: From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling. From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving. From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance. From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming. From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth.
sn12.44:3.7 | S: Rebirth is a requirement for old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress to come to be. | T: From birth as a requisite condition, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.44:3.8 | S: This is the origin of the world. | T: This is the origination of the world.
sn12.44:4.1 | S: And what is the ending of the world? | T: “And what is the ending of the world?
sn12.44:4.2 | S: Eye consciousness arises dependent on the eye and sights. The meeting of the three is contact. | T: Dependent on the eye & forms there arises eye-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.
sn12.44:4.3 | S: Contact is a requirement for feeling. | T: From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling.
sn12.44:4.4 | S: Feeling is a requirement for craving. | T: From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving.
sn12.44:4.5 | S: When that craving fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, grasping ceases. | T: Now, from the remainderless cessation & fading away of that very craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance.
sn12.44:4.6 | S: When grasping ceases, continued existence ceases. … | T: From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.44:4.7 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases. | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.44:4.8 | S: This is the ending of the world. | T: This is the ending of the world.
sn12.44:5.1 | S: Ear consciousness arises dependent on the ear and sounds. … | T: “Dependent on the ear & sounds there arises ear-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.…
sn12.44:5.2 | S: Nose consciousness arises dependent on the nose and smells. … | T: Dependent on the nose & aromas there arises nose-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.…
sn12.44:5.3 | S: Tongue consciousness arises dependent on the tongue and tastes. … | T: Dependent on the tongue & flavors there arises tongue-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.…
sn12.44:5.4 | S: Body consciousness arises dependent on the body and touches. … | T: Dependent on the body & tactile sensations there arises body-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.…
sn12.44:5.5 | S: Mind consciousness arises dependent on the mind and ideas. The meeting of the three is contact. | T: Dependent on the intellect & mental qualities there arises intellect-consciousness. The meeting of the three is contact.
sn12.44:5.6 | S: Contact is a requirement for feeling. | T: From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling.
sn12.44:5.7 | S: Feeling is a requirement for craving. | T: From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving.
sn12.44:5.8 | S: When that craving fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, grasping ceases. | T: Now, from the remainderless cessation & fading away of that very craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance.
sn12.44:5.9 | S: When grasping ceases, continued existence ceases. … | T: From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.44:5.10 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases. | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.44:5.11 | S: This is the ending of the world.” | T: This is the ending of the world.”
sn12.44:5.12 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.46
sn12.46:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Staying near Sāvatthī …
sn12.46:1.2 | S: Then a certain brahmin went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then a certain brahman went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
sn12.46:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
sn12.46:2.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama, are he who does the deed and he who experiences the result one and the same?” | T: “What now, Master Gotama: Is the one who acts the same one who experiences (the results of the act)?”
sn12.46:2.2 | S: “‘He who does the deed and he who experiences the result are one and the same’: this is one extreme, brahmin.” | T: [The Buddha:] “(To say,) ‘The one who acts is the same one who experiences,’ is one extreme.”
sn12.46:3.1 | S: “Then is he who does the deed one and he who experiences the result another?” | T: [The brahman:] “Then, Master Gotama, is the one who acts someone other than the one who experiences?”
sn12.46:3.2 | S: “‘He who does the deed is one and he who experiences the result is another’: this is the second extreme. | T: [The Buddha:] “(To say,) ‘The one who acts is someone other than the one who experiences,’ is the second extreme.
sn12.46:3.3 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way: | T: Avoiding both of these extremes, the Tathāgata teaches the Dhamma via the middle:

