## sn12.52
sn12.52:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn12.52:1.2 | S: “There are things that fuel grasping. When you concentrate on the gratification provided by these things, your craving grows. | T: There the Blessed One said to the monks: “In one who keeps focusing on the allure of clingable phenomena [or: phenomena that offer sustenance = the five aggregates], craving develops.
sn12.52:1.3 | S: Craving is a requirement for grasping. | T: From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance.
sn12.52:1.4 | S: Grasping is a requirement for continued existence. | T: From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming.
sn12.52:1.5 | S: Continued existence is a requirement for rebirth. | T: From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth.
sn12.52:1.6 | S: Rebirth is a requirement for old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress to come to be. | T: From birth as a requisite condition, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.52:1.7 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origin of this entire mass of suffering & stress.
sn12.52:2.1 | S: Suppose a great mass of fire was burning with ten, twenty, thirty, or forty loads of wood. | T: “Just as if a great mass of fire of ten… twenty… thirty or forty cartloads of timber were burning,
sn12.52:2.2 | S: And from time to time someone would toss in dry grass, cow dung, or wood. | T: and into it a man would time & again throw dried grass, dried cow dung, & dried timber,
sn12.52:2.3 | S: Fed and fuelled by that, the great mass of fire would burn for a long time. | T: so that the great mass of fire—thus nourished, thus sustained—would burn for a long, long time.
sn12.52:2.4 | S: In the same way, there are things that fuel grasping. When you concentrate on the gratification provided by these things, your craving grows. | T: In the same way, in one who keeps focusing on the allure of clingable phenomena, craving develops.
sn12.52:2.5 | S: Craving is a requirement for grasping. … | T: From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance. From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming. From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth. From birth as a requisite condition, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.52:2.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origin of this entire mass of suffering & stress.
sn12.52:3.1 | S: There are things that fuel grasping. When you concentrate on the drawbacks of these things, your craving ceases. | T: “Now, in one who keeps focusing on the drawbacks of clingable phenomena, craving ceases.
sn12.52:3.2 | S: When craving ceases, grasping ceases. | T: From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance.
sn12.52:3.3 | S: When grasping ceases, continued existence ceases. | T: From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming.
sn12.52:3.4 | S: When continued existence ceases, rebirth ceases. | T: From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth.
sn12.52:3.5 | S: When rebirth ceases, old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress cease. | T: From the cessation of birth, then aging, illness & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.52:3.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases. | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of suffering & stress.
sn12.52:4.1 | S: Suppose a great mass of fire was burning with ten, twenty, thirty, or forty loads of wood. | T: “Just as if a great mass of fire of ten… twenty… thirty or forty cartloads of timber were burning,
sn12.52:4.2 | S: And no one would toss in dry grass, cow dung, or wood from time to time. | T: into which a man simply would not time & again throw dried grass, dried cow dung, or dried timber,
sn12.52:4.3 | S: As the original fuel is used up and no more is added, the great mass of fire would be extinguished due to not being fed. | T: so that the great mass of fire—its original sustenance being consumed, and no other being offered—would, without nutriment, go out.
sn12.52:4.4 | S: In the same way, there are things that fuel grasping. When you concentrate on the drawbacks of these things, your craving ceases. | T: In the same way, in one who keeps focusing on the drawbacks of clingable phenomena, craving ceases.
sn12.52:4.5 | S: When craving ceases, grasping ceases. … | T: From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance. From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging, illness & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.52:4.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.” | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of suffering & stress.”
sn12.52:4.7 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.55
sn12.55:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Dwelling near Sāvatthī.
sn12.55:1.2 | S: “There are things that fuel grasping. When you concentrate on the gratification provided by these things, your craving grows. | T: “Monks, when one remains focused on the allure of phenomena that offer sustenance for clinging, craving grows.
sn12.55:1.3 | S: Craving is a requirement for grasping. | T: From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance.
sn12.55:1.4 | S: Grasping is a requirement for continued existence. … | T: From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming. From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth. From birth as a requisite condition, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.55:1.5 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.55:2.1 | S: Suppose there was a great tree. | T: “Suppose that there were a great tree.
sn12.55:2.2 | S: And its roots going downwards and across all draw the sap upwards. | T: All its roots growing downward & outward would provide it with water & nutriment,
sn12.55:2.3 | S: Fed and fuelled by that, the great tree would stand for a long time. | T: so that the great tree—thus nourished, thus sustained—would stand for a long, long time.
sn12.55:2.4 | S: In the same way, there are things that fuel grasping. When you concentrate on the gratification provided by these things, your craving grows. | T: “In the same way, monks, when one remains focused on the allure of phenomena that offer sustenance for clinging, craving grows.
sn12.55:2.5 | S: Craving is a requirement for grasping. … | T: From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance. From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming. From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth. From birth as a requisite condition, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.55:2.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.55:3.1 | S: There are things that fuel grasping. When you concentrate on the drawbacks of these things, your craving ceases. | T: “But when one remains focused on the drawbacks of phenomena that offer sustenance for clinging, craving ceases.
sn12.55:3.2 | S: When craving ceases, grasping ceases. | T: From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance.
sn12.55:3.3 | S: When grasping ceases, continued existence ceases. … | T: From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.55:3.4 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases. | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.55:4.1 | S: Suppose there was a great tree. | T: “Suppose there were a great tree.
sn12.55:4.2 | S: Then a person comes along with a spade and basket. | T: A man would come, bringing a shovel & basket,
sn12.55:4.3 | S: They’d cut the tree down at the roots, dig it up, and pull the roots out, down to the fibers and stems. | T: and would cut down the tree at its root. Having cut it at the root, he would dig it up. Having dug it up, he would pull out the roots, down to the rootlets & root fibers.
sn12.55:4.4 | S: They’d cut the tree apart, cut up the parts, and chop it into splinters. They’d dry the splinters in the wind and sun, burn them with fire, and reduce them to ashes. Then they’d winnow the ashes in a strong wind, or float them away down a swift stream. | T: Then he would cut the tree into pieces; having cut the pieces, he would split them; having split them, he would make them into slivers. Having made the slivers, he would dry them in the wind & sun. Having dried them in the wind & sun, he would burn them in a fire. Having burned them in a fire, he would reduce them to ashes. Having reduced them to ashes, he would winnow them before a high wind or let them be washed away by a swift-flowing stream.
sn12.55:4.5 | P: vātātape visosetvā agginā ḍaheyya, agginā ḍahetvā masiṁ kareyya, masiṁ karitvā mahāvāte vā ophuṇeyya nadiyā vā sīghasotāya pavāheyya. | T: (none)
sn12.55:4.6 | S: In this way the great tree is cut off at the root, made like a palm stump, obliterated, and unable to arise in the future. | T: Thus the great tree, cut at the root, would be made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising.
sn12.55:4.7 | S: In the same way, there are things that fuel grasping. When you concentrate on the drawbacks of these things, your craving ceases. | T: “In the same way, monks, when one remains focused on the drawbacks of phenomena that offer sustenance for clinging, craving ceases.
sn12.55:4.8 | S: When craving ceases, grasping ceases. | T: From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance.
sn12.55:4.9 | S: When grasping ceases, continued existence ceases. … | T: From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.55:4.10 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.” | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.”
sn12.55:4.11 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.61
sn12.61:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn12.61:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. … | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn12.61:1.3 | S: “Mendicants, an unlearned ordinary person might become disillusioned, dispassionate, and freed from this body made up of the four principal states. | T: There he addressed the monks, “Monks, an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person might grow disenchanted with this body composed of the four great elements, might grow dispassionate toward it, might gain release from it.
sn12.61:1.4 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn12.61:1.5 | S: This body made up of the four principal states is seen to accumulate and disperse, to be taken up and laid to rest. | T: Because the growth & decline, the taking up & putting down of this body composed of the four great elements are apparent.
sn12.61:1.6 | S: That’s why an unlearned ordinary person might become disillusioned, dispassionate, and freed from it. | T: Thus the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person might grow disenchanted, might grow dispassionate, might gain release there.
sn12.61:2.1 | S: But an unlearned ordinary person is unable to become disillusioned, dispassionate, or freed from that which is called ‘mind’ and also ‘sentience’ and also ‘consciousness’. | T: “But as for what’s called ‘mind,’ ‘intellect,’ or ‘consciousness,’ the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person is unable to grow disenchanted with it, unable to grow dispassionate toward it, unable to gain release from it.
sn12.61:2.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn12.61:2.3 | S: Because for a long time they’ve been attached to it, thought of it as their own, and mistaken it: | T: For a long time this has been relished, appropriated, and grasped by the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person as,
sn12.61:2.4 | S: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self.’ | T: ‘This is me, this is my self, this is what I am.’
sn12.61:2.5 | S: That’s why an unlearned ordinary person is unable to become disillusioned, dispassionate, or freed from it. | T: Thus the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person is unable to grow disenchanted with it, unable to grow dispassionate toward it, unable to gain release from it.
sn12.61:3.1 | S: But an unlearned ordinary person would be better off taking this body made up of the four principal states to be their self, rather than the mind. | T: “It would be better for the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person to hold to the body composed of the four great elements, rather than the mind, as the self.
sn12.61:3.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn12.61:3.3 | S: This body made up of the four principal states is seen to last for a year, or for two, three, four, five, ten, twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, or a hundred years, or even longer. | T: Because this body composed of the four great elements is seen standing for a year, two years, three, four, five, ten, twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, a hundred years or more.
sn12.61:4.1 | S: But that which is called ‘mind’ and also ‘sentience’ and also ‘consciousness’ arises as one thing and ceases as another all day and all night. | T: But what’s called ‘mind,’ ‘intellect,’ or ‘consciousness’ by day and by night arises as one thing and ceases as another.
sn12.61:4.2 | S: It’s like a monkey moving through the forest. It grabs hold of one branch, lets it go, and grabs another; then it lets that go and grabs yet another. | T: Just as a monkey, swinging through a forest wilderness, grabs a branch. Letting go of that, it grabs another branch. Letting go of that, it grabs another one. Letting go of that, it grabs another one.
sn12.61:4.3 | S: In the same way, that which is called ‘mind’ and also ‘sentience’ and also ‘consciousness’ arises as one thing and ceases as another all day and all night. | T: In the same way, what’s called ‘mind,’ ‘intellect,’ or ‘consciousness’ by day and by night arises as one thing and ceases as another.
sn12.61:5.1 | S: In this case, a learned noble disciple carefully and rationally applies the mind to dependent origination itself: | T: “The instructed disciple of the noble ones, (however,) attends carefully & appropriately right there at the dependent co-arising:
sn12.61:5.2 | S: ‘So, when this exists, this comes to be; due to the arising of this, this arises. | T: “‘When this is, that is. “‘From the arising of this comes the arising of that.
sn12.61:5.3 | S: When this doesn’t exist, this doesn’t come to be; due to the cessation of this, this ceases. That is: | T: “‘When this isn’t, that isn’t. “‘From the cessation of this comes the cessation of that. “‘In other words:
sn12.61:5.4 | S: Ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: “‘From ignorance as a requisite condition come fabrications.
sn12.61:5.5 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: “‘From fabrications as a requisite condition comes consciousness. “‘From consciousness as a requisite condition comes name-&-form. “‘From name-&-form as a requisite condition come the six sense media. “‘From the six sense media as a requisite condition comes contact. “‘From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling. “‘From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving. “‘From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance. “‘From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming. “‘From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth. “‘From birth as a requisite condition, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn12.61:5.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn12.61:5.7 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: “‘Now from the remainderless fading & cessation of that very ignorance comes the cessation of fabrications.
sn12.61:5.8 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: From the cessation of fabrications comes the cessation of consciousness. From the cessation of consciousness comes the cessation of name-&-form. From the cessation of name-&-form comes the cessation of the six sense media. From the cessation of the six sense media comes the cessation of contact. From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of feeling. From the cessation of feeling comes the cessation of craving. From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance. From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging-&-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn12.61:5.9 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.’ | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.’
sn12.61:6.1 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: “Seeing thus, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with form, disenchanted with feeling, disenchanted with perception, disenchanted with fabrications, disenchanted with consciousness.
sn12.61:6.2 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. When desire fades away they’re freed. When they’re freed, they know they’re freed. | T: Disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate. Through dispassion, he is released. With release, there is the knowledge, ‘Released.’
sn12.61:6.3 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’” | T: He discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world.’”
sn12.61:6.4 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)


